    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Non-healing lesion of the left lower lip for about three months: a 1.2 cm firm, crusted ulcer on the vermilion that bleeds when touched, on a background of dry, scaly lips from years of outdoor work; no numbness of the lip or chin and no palpable neck nodes. Suspicious for squamous cell carcinoma; incisional biopsy taken today and otolaryngology consultation booked</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>K13.0</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem linked to his documented lip cancer; superseded by the biopsy diagnosis.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Chronic coronary disease with stable exertional angina: tightness in the chest when walking uphill or carrying loads for about a year, relieved by two or three minutes of rest, never at rest; heart attack eight years ago, treated in hospital, with no regular care or medicines since. Resting ECG: sinus rhythm with Q waves in the inferior leads. Synthetic modern episode linked to his documented heart attacks</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I25.119</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding of the documented heart disease.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Squamous cell carcinoma of the left lower lip (external vermilion): invasive, well-differentiated keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma on incisional biopsy; 1.2 cm, no palpable neck nodes; clinical stage T1 N0 (AJCC 8th edition, lip and oral cavity). Synthetic modern episode linked to his documented lip cancer</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>C00.1</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis; the cited sources do not record the type of his lip cancer.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

