    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Squamous cell carcinoma of the left lower lip (C00.1)</content>. Biopsy-proven invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the lower lip, 1.2 cm, with no palpable neck nodes: clinical T1 N0. Dr. Ferrante recommends V-excision with 5 to 10 mm margins and frozen-section margin control under local anesthesia, a contrast CT of the neck first, and no elective neck dissection if the final depth of invasion is small. He agreed. [S]<list><item>Contrast CT of the neck next week.</item><item>V-excision in two weeks.</item><item>Continue aspirin and metoprolol through surgery.</item><item>Lip balm with sunscreen and a hat.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Non-healing lesion of the left lower lip (K13.0)</content>. Superseded by the biopsy diagnosis of squamous cell carcinoma. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic coronary disease with stable angina (I25.119)</content>. Echocardiogram: left ventricular ejection fraction 55% with an old inferior wall motion abnormality and no significant valve disease. Angina is stable and infrequent on the new medicines. A lip excision under local anesthesia is a low-risk operation; with stable symptoms, normal heart function and no heart failure, no further cardiac testing is needed before it. Blood pressure 130/80, at the edge of the goal. [S]<list><item>Continue aspirin 81 mg, atorvastatin 80 mg and metoprolol succinate 25 mg.</item><item>Nitroglycerin instructions reviewed.</item><item>Lipid panel in about four weeks.</item><item>Cardiac rehabilitation once the lip has healed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Examination of the lips, mouth, and neck</td><td>At diagnosis, then on the surveillance schedule</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1.2 cm lower lip ulcer; no neck nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Pathology: type, margins, depth of invasion, perineural and lymphovascular invasion</td><td>Biopsy before treatment; full pathology after excision</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Incisional biopsy: invasive, well-differentiated keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Imaging of the primary site and neck</td><td>Before treatment as indicated; afterwards only for symptoms or findings</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Contrast CT of the neck next week</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sun protection of the lips and skin</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Lip balm with SPF 30 and a hat advised</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Tobacco and alcohol use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Tobacco-free for eight years; AUDIT-C 2 last week</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure (goal below 130/80)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>130/80</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight, BMI, and physical activity</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>93.8 kg, BMI 27.4; walks most days but stops on hills</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Angina frequency and nitroglycerin use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One episode on a hill this week, eased by rest; no nitroglycerin</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and side effects: aspirin, high-intensity statin, beta-blocker</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>All taken daily for a week; mild leg tiredness on metoprolol, easing</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel</td><td>4 to 12 weeks after starting or changing lipid therapy, then every 3 to 12 months; goal LDL below 55 mg/dL</td><td>Due</td><td>LDL 158 mg/dL off treatment last week; recheck in about four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>Baseline when heart function is unknown; repeat only for a change in symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ejection fraction 55%; old inferior wall motion abnormality</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Perioperative cardiac assessment</td><td>Before noncardiac surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Low-risk surgery under local anesthesia; stable angina, normal ejection fraction; no further testing; aspirin and beta-blocker continued</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Stress testing or angiography</td><td>Only for new or worsening symptoms despite medical therapy</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Stable angina responding to medical therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Otolaryngology (head and neck surgery)</td><td>At diagnosis of a lip cancer</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Ferrante today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Cardiac rehabilitation</td><td>After a heart attack or for stable angina: up to 36 sessions</td><td>Due</td><td>To start once the lip has healed</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Lip cancer removed with clear margins and no recurrence on surveillance through four months. In progress; target 1951-10-26 [S]</item><item>LDL cholesterol below 55 mg/dL on secondary prevention. In progress; target 1951-09-21 [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg. In progress; target 1951-09-21 [S]</item><item>Complete cardiac rehabilitation and walk uphill without angina. In progress; target 1951-10-26 [S]</item><item>Stay tobacco-free and protect his lips and skin from the sun. In progress; target 1951-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Head and Neck Cancers (cancer of the lip and oral cavity) (2026); 2023 AHA/ACC/ACCP/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease (2023); 2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol (2018); 2024 AHA/ACC/ACS/ASNC/HRS/SCA/SCCT/SCMR/SVM Guideline for Perioperative Cardiovascular Management for Noncardiac Surgery (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, ischemic heart disease monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: CT of the neck next week; surgery in two weeks. Visit two weeks after surgery with pathology and a lipid panel. Next scheduled visit 1951-07-13: two weeks after excision of the lip cancer: pathology, wound check, surveillance plan, lipid recheck, and cardiac rehabilitation. [S]</paragraph></text>

